{{also|crevasse}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|lang=enm|crevice}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|lang=fro|crevace}}, from {{term|lang=fro|crever||to break, burst}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|lang=la|crepare||to break, burst, crack}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkɹɛvɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[narrow]] [[crack]] or [[fissure]], as in a [[rock]] or [[wall]].
#* Tennyson
#*: The mouse, / Behind the moldering wainscot, shrieked, / Or from the '''crevice''' peered about.
#* William Butler Yeats
#*: I can't tell you how urbane and sprightly the old poll parrot was; and {{...}} not a pocket, not a '''crevice''', of pomp, humbug, respectability in him: he was fresh as a daisy.

